// THE PARALLEL METHOD (30 Sep 2026). Argument and mechanism read together: a claim that states
// a step enters the argument as the support of a claim it bears on, or through a BRIDGE whose
// inference line names the scheme. Reconstructed in the trial that made this the method in force
// (1 Oct 2026); the author judged it the better of the two readings of this paper.
//
// Mechanism pass, profile 1.11 (30 Sep 2026), done in the main session. The paper describes the
// health system in verbs -- flows, waiting, discharging, cascading -- and itself cites Arthur on
// economics "with nouns only, rather than verbs" (p. 355), so the states keep the text's verbs.
// Means- and values-improvement are declared a cycle that does not come to rest: "continual and
// iterative" ([Continual and iterative], p. 363). What makes the system up is marked with
// `constitutes:`, never as steps.
//
// Top-level form: conductive. Four convergent strands (flows, values beyond cost-effectiveness,
// cost-effectiveness analysis as one tool, iterative improvement) are weighed together against
// a reported rival, the move to comprehensive cost-effectiveness analysis, which the paper refutes.

# The thesis {isGroup: true}

[Holistic improvement]: "health systems should move from simple and atomistic approaches to measuring effectiveness to approaches that are holistic both in focusing on performance at the level of the health system as a whole, and also in incorporating a wider range of ethical concerns in thinking about what makes a health system good"
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 351", note: "The paper's stated thesis, from the abstract. Its two arms, system-level performance and a wider range of ethical concerns, are carried by the flow-centric and values strands below."}
    <+ [Flow-centric shift]
    <+ [Values beyond cost-effectiveness]
    <+ [CEA as one tool]
    <+ [Iterative improvement]
    <- [Go comprehensive]

# Against cost-effectiveness-centred improvement {isGroup: true}

<Four assumptions argument>: Centring improvement on cost-effectiveness is sensible only if four assumptions hold, and each is implausible in a range of real-world contexts; so cost-effectiveness analysis should be reframed as one tool among many. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 360"}

<Four assumptions argument>

(1) [CEA sensible only if four hold]: "centring improvement attempts on improving cost-effectiveness is a sensible strategy only if measurabillty, single synoptic decision procedure, external validity, and static ranking all hold" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 360", note: "'measurabillty' is the published misspelling, kept as printed."}
(2) [Four assumptions implausible]: "each of these assumptions prove to be implausible in a range of real-world contexts" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 360", note: "Scope kept: implausible in a range of real-world contexts, not in every context."}
-----
(3) [CEA as one tool]: Tools such as cost-effectiveness analysis should not be abandoned but reframed "as one among a plurality of tools by which health system performance can be measured and means-improved" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 360"}

[CEA as one tool]
    <+ [Pragmatic tool]: Conceptions of improvement that use cost-effectiveness can "adopt a view of cost-effectiveness analysis that sees it as a pragmatically useful, but imperfect tool, rather than the only ethically defensible approach to health system improvement" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 355"}
        <+ [Measurability doubtful]
        <+ [SSDP controversial]
    <+ [Constrained and judicious]: "where cost-effectiveness analysis is used, it should be in a constrained and judicious way" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 356"}
        <+ [Not testable once and for all]: If external validity and static ranking are questionable, "it is also questionable to assume that the cost-effectiveness of particular interventions can be tested centrally once and for all" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 356", note: "The antecedent compresses the author's 'if it is admitted that it is questionable to assume that cost-effectiveness analyses have external validity, and ... that prioritisation rankings will often be dynamic'; the quoted consequent follows in the same sentence."}
            <+ [EV and SR fail]
    <+ [Not only CEA]: "it would be a mistake to think that successful means-improvement occurs only where cost-effectiveness analysis is used" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 361"}
        <+ [Sub-elements are improved in turn]
        <+ [Uniform standards the exception]

[Sub-elements are improved in turn]: "Systems, and also technologies more generally, are typically composed of sub-elements which are themselves technologies or systems, and this encapsulation can be several layers deep. Sub-elements can themselves be means-improved, and this iterates down to sub-sub-processes and beyond" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 361", constitutes: {from: subsystems, to: nhs, extent: partial, whole: organised, basis: asserted}}

[Four assumptions implausible]
    <+ [Measurability doubtful]: "it is neither obvious that health and illness are cardinally measurable, nor there is any alternative specification of the aim of health policy that is cardinally measurable either" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 354", note: "Measurability: that whatever makes healthcare valuable can be measured on a cardinal scale, with numerical comparisons across all states."}
        <+ [Illnesses differ]: "influenza, depression, spina bifida, herpes, and rheumatoid arthritis are all ways of being ill, but they each affect us in very different ways" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 354"}
        <+ [Plural valid measures]: "it is much less plausible to think that there is a single and uniquely correct way of measuring health or wellbeing, than a plurality of different valid ways of so doing" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 354"}
            <+ [QALY choices contestable]: The QALY's methodology embeds value judgements, and "in each case there are reasonable alternatives to the decisions that were taken" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 354", note: "Hedge kept: the author says none of these decisions is shown to be indefensible, only that reasonable alternatives exist. The same is said of the DALY."}
    <+ [SSDP controversial]: "Single synoptic decision procedure is widely agreed to be controversial." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 355"}
        <+ [Unit weighting unclear]: "it is unclear whether one unit (e.g. one QALY) should count the same regardless of the disease it arises from, how badly off the patients are who are affected, and how significant the benefit is to each individual who would receive it" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 355"}
        <+ [Other values]: "there is a range of other values that are often thought to be relevant to health systems improvement, such as inclusion, and environmental sustainability that are only indirectly connected to health achievement" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 355"}
        <+ [Daniels on disagreement]: Prominent scholars, including Norman Daniels, argue "that it is not possible to resolve a range of underlying ethical disagreements about tradeoffs in resource allocation through philosophical reasoning alone" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 355", note: "The author reports this and draws only a conditional from it ('If they are correct, a turn to procedural reason is required'); the map records that scholars argue it, not that it is true."}
    <+ [EV and SR fail]

[EV and SR fail]: "neither external validity nor static ranking is correct within large-scale and interconnected health systems" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 354", note: "Scope kept: within large-scale and interconnected health systems."}
    <+ [EV usually false]: "It is a substantive, and usually false, assumption that the effects (and hence cost-effectiveness) of an intervention are atomic, i.e. that they are the same everywhere the intervention is undertaken, rather than shifting in response to changes elsewhere in health systems." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 356", note: "External validity: cost-effectiveness assessed in one context transfers without significant difficulty to the policymaker's own. The general step; adherence and the cataract service are its cases.", causes: {from: syschange, to: effects, sign: which, scope: general, basis: example, chain: dynamic}}
        <+ [Adherence]: "The effectiveness (and cost-effectiveness) of a drug in practice depends on, among other things, on the level of support that patients have in adhering to treatment." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 356", causes: {from: adherence, to: drugce, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: dynamic}}
        <+ [Trial gap]: "there may be a significant gap between the effects of an intervention in an experimental context, and the effects that the same intervention will have in a real-world context" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 355"}
    <+ [Rankings change]: "it is common, rather than rare, for circumstances to change in ways that require rankings delivered in prioritisation processes to be reconfigured within rounds of resource allocation, rather than only at the beginning of such a period" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 356", note: "Static ranking: that prioritisation rankings will not change unexpectedly over the period governed by the allocation.", causes: {from: circumstances, to: ranking, sign: which, scope: general, basis: example, chain: dynamic}}
        <+ [Operating parameters]: "If the system needs to start operating outside of these parameters, then this may make a significant difference to what calibration of services will be overall most cost-effective." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 356", note: "The parameters are assumed patient flows, e.g. 300 beds sufficing, or an A&E department processing no more than 40 patients an hour.", causes: {from: beyond, to: calibration, sign: which, basis: example, hedged: true, chain: dynamic}}
        <+ [Fair ordering decays]: "A prioritisation ordering that was fair may not remain fair as patients’ conditions change." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 356", causes: {from: change, to: fairorder, sign: "-", on: persistence, basis: asserted, hedged: true, chain: dynamic}}

<Effects shift with the system>: In the cataract service and the ambulance cascade, what an intervention achieves shifts with how long patients have waited and with what happens elsewhere in the system. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 358-360", note: "The text's own use of the two cases: 'By looking at a case study on cataract waiting lists, and one on cascading harms caused by ambulance waiting times, I argue that neither external validity nor static ranking is correct' (p. 354)."}

(1) [Waiting and worsening feed each other]
(2) [Cataract service]
(3) [Cascading scarcity]
(4) [Discharge to ambulance cascade]
-- From a mechanism against a theory {uses: [1, 2, 3, 4]} --
(5) [EV and SR fail]

[Waiting and worsening feed each other]: "Cases become more time-consuming to treat (and more prone to complications) as cataracts worsen, and cataracts worsen as patients wait longer for treatment." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 359", causes: [{from: cataractwait, to: worsen, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: cataract}, {from: worsen, to: casetime, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: cataract}]}

# The NHS and the comprehensive alternative {isGroup: true}

<Partial CEA criticised>: The NHS's partial use of cost-effectiveness analysis has let demand outrun capacity, harmfully, and the failures follow from the approach; allowing such a gap is irresponsible, so the partial approach is open to ethical criticism. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 357-358"}

<Partial CEA criticised>

(1) [Congestion]: The NHS's current partial approach to cost-effectiveness "has not been sufficient to prevent services from becoming congested and waiting times rising inexorably in ways that are harmful for patients" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 363", note: "The framing words are the reconstructor's; the quoted span is from the Conclusion, where the subject is 'the current partial approach to cost-effectiveness'.", causes: {from: congestion, to: harm, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: congestion}}
(2) [Failures follow from approach]: "these failures are not incidental, but follow from the approach adopted" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 354", causes: {from: partialcea, to: congestion, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: congestion}}
(3) [Irresponsible gap]: "It is irresponsible for policymakers to allow a systemic gap to appear between the demands that individuals can make under the system, and the system’s ability to meet these demands." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 358"}
-----
(4) [Partial CEA open to criticism]: "To the extent that partial cost-effectiveness analysis leads to such problems in practice, it can and should be ethically criticized." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 358"}
    +> [Go comprehensive]

[Congestion]
    <+ [Handover delays contributed to deaths]
    <+ [Worst waiting lists]: "As of the beginning of 2023, the NHS was facing worse waiting lists for scheduled operations than at any point in its history" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 358"}
    +> [Flow-centric shift]

[Handover delays contributed to deaths]: A report of the Association of Ambulance Chief Executives concluded "that each month over 25,000 patients were waiting for more than four times the target handover time of 15 min, and that these delays had contributed to deaths" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 360", note: "Footnote 7, attached to the ambulance-waiting sentence; the report is the author's evidence, and he relies on it.", causes: {from: ambwait, to: harm, sign: "+", basis: statistics, chain: cascade}}

[Failures follow from approach]
    <+ [Mixed economy]: The NHS "runs a mixed economy in which only a minority of decisions is shaped by formal measurement of cost-effectiveness" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 354"}
        <+ [Satisficing not maximising]: NICE "operates a satisficing model of cost-effectiveness rather than a maximising one" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 357"}
    <+ <NICE threshold argument>
    <+ [No stopping mechanism]: "The system as a whole has no very clear mechanisms for stopping doing interventions that are not cost-effective." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 358"}

<NICE threshold argument>: New interventions are admitted at a threshold well above what a QALY costs within the NHS, so many approvals are expected to cost more health than they create. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 357-358"}

<NICE threshold argument>

(1) [Threshold]: Unless special features justify paying more, "a new intervention should not usually cost more than £30,000 per QALY" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 357"}
(2) [Claxton estimate]: "Claxton et al. (2015) calculated that the most likely value for the cost it takes to create one QALY within the NHS is £12,936" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 357"}
-----
(3) [Net QALY losses]: "many of the drugs approved by NICE would be expected to lead to losses of health benefits that are larger than those they create" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 358", note: "Hedged in the text: 'would be expected', and 'if the cost-effectiveness calculations are correct'. The author adds that such approvals may have other virtues, such as access to more expensive treatments with genuine benefits.", causes: {from: partialcea, to: qalyloss, sign: "+", basis: model, hedged: true, chain: congestion}}

<Excess demand's consequences>: Letting in more demand than the system can serve congests it, raises unmet need and pushes those who can pay into private care, none of it intended; so allowing the gap is irresponsible. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 357-358"}

(1) [Excess demand congests the system]
(2) [Other sequelae of excess demand]
(3) [Implicit rationing is the unwanted effect of excess demand]: "In many cases, implicit rationing can occur without explicit decision or endorsement: queuing and congestion are often simply the unwanted net effect of excess demand within parts of the system." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 357", causes: {from: gap, to: congestion, sign: "+", basis: asserted, attribution: {type: inadvertent, by: policymakers}, chain: congestion}}
