<Cataract argument>: Where demand outstrips capacity for a progressive condition, first come, first served lets patients deteriorate while they wait; so a cataract service that makes people wait is both less cost-effective and less ethically defensible. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 358-359"}

<Cataract argument>

(1) [FCFS inferior]: "If demand systematically outstrips ability to meet it, and those waiting become significantly worse (and more difficult to treat) while they are waiting, then first-come first-served is ethically inferior to a system of explicit prioritisation based on criteria that allows capacity to be matched to eligible need." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 358"}
(2) [FCFS lets progressive patients deteriorate first]: "Where first-come, first-served is used to manage access to treatments for progressive conditions, and demand systematically outstrips ability to meet it, then no patients will reach the front" "of the queue until their condition has already deteriorated significantly and this avoidable loss of health will grow as the queue lengthens." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 358", note: "The general mechanism the conditional (1) needs. One sentence of the source, quoted in two spans because it runs across the page break between 'front' and 'of the queue'.", causes: [{from: fcfs, to: worsen, sign: "+", jointly: [gap], basis: asserted, scope: general, chain: cataract}, {from: waiting, to: loss, sign: "+", basis: asserted, scope: general, chain: cataract}]}
    <+ [First come, first served lets waits grow]
(3) [Cataract progressive]: "Cataract is a progressive condition. The longer it is left before an operation, the greater the complexity of the operation and the greater likelihood of complications." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 359", causes: {from: cataractwait, to: casetime, sign: "+", basis: asserted, via: worsen, chain: cataract}}
(4) [Waiting and worsening feed each other]
(5) [A backlog outruns the capacity that held it]
(6) [Cataract waits growing]: The mean wait for cataract surgery "was 278 days (nearly 40 weeks) in 2021", against an 18-week target {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 359"}
(7) [Waiting diminishes patients' lives]
