-- From consequences {uses: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]} --
(7) [Public deliberation decides]: Where cost savings from a reform improve care elsewhere, "it will be a matter for public deliberation whether the reforms are in conflict with goods internal to the practice of healthcare, or rather allow for a better specification of them" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 363", note: "Drawn from the author's worked case of a ward where each clinician is given more patients. 'There may not be universal answers to such questions, and deliberative communities may find different equilibria.'", causes: {from: deliberation, to: valuesimp, sign: which, basis: asserted, chain: improvement}}

[Pressures break staff]: Rising sick leave and turnover, the loss of a personal relationship with patients, workplace stress, and the moral distress of subordinating medicine's internal goods all contribute to staff moral injury and breakdown. {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence", pinpoint: "pp. 362-363", source: "staff moral injury and breakdown", note: "The text names each of these pressures (pp. 362-363) and names moral injury and breakdown as a cost a health system cannot price in, but does not say in words that each leads to it. The author confirms that this is what he intended the reader to understand, and that the words may not communicate it (James Wilson, 30 Sep 2026).", causes: [{from: turnover, to: injury, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: staff}, {from: relationship, to: injury, sign: "-", basis: asserted, chain: staff}, {from: stress, to: injury, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: staff}, {from: distress, to: injury, sign: "+", basis: asserted, chain: staff}]}

[Ward reform strains clinicians]: "These reforms also make it more difficult for clinicians to establish a personal relationship with patients, and somewhat increase workplace stress." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 363", note: "The text's worked supposition: a means-improvement that raises a ward's cost-effectiveness by giving each clinician more patients.", causes: [{from: reform, to: relationship, sign: "-", basis: asserted, design: illustration, chain: staff}, {from: reform, to: stress, sign: "+", basis: asserted, design: illustration, chain: staff}]}

[Overtime holds quality up, unsustainably]: "Conscientious clinicians may respond to such pressures unsustainably by attempting to maintain quality of care through creeping increases of unpaid overtime." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 363", causes: [{from: stress, to: overtime, sign: "+", basis: asserted, hedged: true, design: illustration, chain: staff}, {from: overtime, to: quality, sign: "+", on: persistence, basis: asserted, hedged: true, design: illustration, chain: staff}]}

[Limited care distresses good clinicians]: "values at the heart of the social practice thus may make it morally distressing for clinicians to practice in a way that does not allow them to provide good enough care to their patients." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 362", note: "Marked first as norms -> distress, which composed with the sidelining step into a route saying that sidelining the norms LOWERS distress -- the opposite of the text. The cause is practising without being able to give good enough care; the norms are what make it distressing, so they are the joint condition.", causes: {from: limitcare, to: distress, sign: "+", jointly: [norms], hedged: true, basis: asserted, chain: staff}}

# Improvement as continual and iterative {isGroup: true}

[Iterative improvement]: "we should shift our focus to means-improvement and values-improvement as iterative and often every-day processes by which health systems are improved" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 363"}
    <+ [Entangled]
    <+ [Everyday means-improvement]
    <+ <Democratic open-endedness>

[Entangled]: "we should think of their relationship as one that is often entangled and reciprocal, rather than discrete and hierarchical" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 360", note: "'their' = means-improvement and values-improvement. Means-improvement reconfigures flows to realise a system's values; values-improvement specifies and reconciles those values. Hedge kept: 'often'."}
    <+ [Means presupposes values]: "Means-improvement thus presupposes an account of values-improvement" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 361"}
        <+ [Values set the target]
        <+ [Disparities call for new means]
        <+ [Success relative to values]: "A set of changes to a health system will count a successful means-improvement only if it allows the system as a whole to move in the direction of its values" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 361"}
    <+ [Means shapes values]: "Means-improvement also shapes values-improvement, both by opening up new possibilities, and by problematising existing values." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 361", causes: {from: meansimp, to: valuesimp, sign: which, basis: example, chain: improvement}}
        <+ [Trusted Research Environments]: "improvements in anonymisation technologies, and in building Trusted Research Environments make it far easier to do necessary research and planning using linked patient level data in a way that is compatible with strong protection of patient privacy" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 361", causes: {from: tre, to: reconcile, sign: "+", on: possibility, basis: example, chain: improvement}}
        <+ [Infeasible visions prompt rethink]: "if a system is unable adequately to deliver the vision specified by a process of values-improvement, even after diligent means-improvement, then this may lead to a requirement for further values-improvement" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 361", causes: {from: infeasible, to: valuesimp, sign: "+", basis: asserted, hedged: true, chain: improvement}}

[Values set the target]: "values-improvement specifies the target for means-improvement at any particular time" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 361", causes: {from: valuesimp, to: meansimp, sign: which, basis: asserted, chain: improvement}}

[Disparities call for new means]: "if a values-improvement process leads a health system to place greater emphasis on reducing health disparities between different ethnic groups, then this may require a series of means-improvements such as to the ways in which ethnicity data is coded and recorded" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 361", causes: {from: valuesimp, to: meansimp, sign: which, hedged: true, basis: example, chain: improvement}}

[Everyday means-improvement]: "we should move away from thinking of means-improvement as an unusual activity that can only be undertaken by highly skilled professionals in special circumstances, towards thinking of it as an everyday activity" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 361"}
    <+ [Uniform standards the exception]: "measuring and improving all component elements in a system against the same set of values and the same standards of efficiency (as in cost-effectiveness analysis) is the exception, rather than the rule in means-improvement" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 361"}
    <- [Disrupted flows]: "there are a variety of scenarios such as the outbreak of a pandemic, or responding to a major terrorist incident or natural disaster, where habitual flows are so disrupted or overwhelmed that means-improvement may not be feasible" #conceded {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 361", note: "Footnote 9: means-improvement presupposes a degree of stability that day-to-day public policy can assume but some scenarios lack."}

<Democratic open-endedness>: If a democracy's goals must stay open to public debate, open-ended goals are a virtue of democratically controlled institutions, and improvement of both kinds should be expected to be continual. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 363"}

<Democratic open-endedness>

(1) [Democratic goals open-ended]: "There are reasons to think that a democracy’s goals must, as a matter of principle, remain open-ended and subject to public debate unless the goals are for specific reasons excluded from reconsideration." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 363", note: "Hedged in the text, and the conclusion is introduced with 'If so'. The map keeps the hedge in the premise rather than asserting it outright."}
(2) [Health goals set democratically]: The goals of healthcare institutions are set in part through "democratic deliberation" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 354", note: "From the roadmap: 'the role of democratic deliberation in setting the goals for healthcare institutions'."}
-----
(3) [Continual and iterative]: "open-endedness of goals is a virtue rather than a vice of democratically controlled institutions, and we should expect both means-improvement and values-improvement to be continual and iterative" {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 363"}

[Continual and iterative]
    <+ [Imaginative failure]: "It may be a sign of imaginative failure rather than argumentative success if it comes to seem that the goals of a health system have all been fully specified and no longer need further debate." {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 363"}
        <+ <Closed goals become fixations>
    <- [Not everything at once]: "not everything can or should be called into question at the same time" #conceded {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 363", note: "The author's own limit on open-endedness: even if all democratic ends are up for renegotiation, they are not all open at once."}
    <- [Static internal morality]

<Closed goals become fixations>: Ends and values cut off from the ongoing process become fixations, and goals taken as fully specified are cut off from it; so goals that seem fully specified are a sign of imaginative failure. {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 363"}

(1) [Dewey on fixations]: Dewey: "All ends and values that are cut off from the ongoing process become arrests, fixations." {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 363", causes: {from: cutoff, to: fixation, sign: which, on: character, basis: testimony, chain: improvement}}
(2) [Closing the goals cuts them off]: Where a health system's goals are taken as fully specified and values-improvement stops, its ends and values are cut off from the ongoing process of improvement. {fidelity: "imputation", warrant: "enthymeme", note: "The step the inference from Dewey to imaginative failure relies on, and no claim states: goals 'fully specified and no longer need[ing] further debate' (p. 363) are ends cut off from the ongoing process of means- and values-improvement.", causes: {from: valuesimp, to: cutoff, sign: "-", basis: asserted, chain: improvement}}
-----
(3) [Imaginative failure]

[Static internal morality]: Some scholars suggest that medicine has an internal morality which "provides an objective and static basis for the goals of medicine, rather than these being subject to debate and change" #reported {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 362", note: "Footnote 11, citing Pellegrino (2001); on such a view 'there may be purposes that are excluded by physicians’ integrity, such as participating in state executions' (Miller and Brody 1995)."}
    <- [Practice goals change]: MacIntyre himself holds that "practices never have a goal or goals fixed for all time" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 362"}
    <- [Allocation not settled]: Even if medicine has an internal morality, "it is not plausible to think that questions of how to allocate scarce healthcare resources will be settled either by the kind of social practice healthcare is, or by the internal morality of medicine" {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 362", note: "The author grants the exclusion of some purposes ('Even if this is correct') and denies that it reaches questions of allocation, which is where the static-basis claim would bear on this paper."}
